Here is a Java application that generates and displays all prime numbers in the range of 1 to n, where n is a specified integer.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class PrimeNumbersGenerator {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int n = 20; // Define the range 1 to n
        List<Integer> primeNumbers = generatePrimes(n);
        System.out.println("Prime numbers between 1 and " + n + ": " + primeNumbers);
    }

    public static List<Integer> generatePrimes(int n) {
        List<Integer> primes = new ArrayList<Integer>();
        boolean[] isPrime = new boolean[n + 1];
        for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
            isPrime[i] = true;
        }
        for (int p = 2; p * p <= n; p++) {
            if (isPrime[p]) {
                for (int i = p * 2; i <= n; i += p) {
                    isPrime[i] = false;
                }
            }
        }
        for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
            if (isPrime[i]) {
                primes.add(i);
            }
        }
        return primes;
    }

}
```

In this application, we first define the range of numbers 1 to n. We then call the `generatePrimes` function to generate and return a list of all prime numbers in the range. The `generatePrimes` function uses a basic algorithm to determine if a number is prime: it iterates over all numbers in the range and marks all non-prime numbers as false in the `isPrime` array. Finally, it adds all prime numbers to the list and returns it.

When we run this application, we should get the following output:

```bash
Prime numbers between 1 and 20: [2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13, 17, 19]
```

This output shows that there are 8 prime numbers between 1 and 20: 2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13, 17, and 19.

